\begin{abstract}
Europa, the icy moon of Jupiter (isnt ganymede icy?), is a prime target in the search for extraterrestrial life.
It is thought to have a deep salty ocean under a thick layer of ice, with many of the conditions that are thought to have led to the emergence of life on Earth. There have been many plans() to land small scientific payloads on the surface of Europa. The long transit times and extremely low science payloads have stunted the development and funding of these proposals. More advanced proposals for the exploration of Europa, such as cyrobots have not been developed extensively  as the possibility of landing heavy machinery on the surface of Europa has been considered impossible in the near term. The development of the Starship rocket enables vastly higher payloads to be delivered to Europa, faster and cheaper than before. This paper outlines the possibility of Starship delivering decatonne scientific payloads directly to the surface of Europa 2.5 years after launch from Earth. 
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\subsection{History and motivation}
Europa, the second Moon of Jupiter has been investigated remotely by the Pioneer missions, the Voyager missions Galileo and will be further remotely explored by the Europa Clipper. There has, however, been no mission to the surface, nor is one under development by NASA. This is partially because 

Historical planning for missions to Europa has been heavily constrained by the low mass and lengthy transit times required for spacecraft to reach Jovian space on historical expendable rockets.

With the advent of reusable super heavy lift launch vehicles, it is feasible to send much heavier payloads on much faster trajectories. 




