\section{Trajectory}
\subsection*{Earth to Jupiter}
As is standard in preliminary Jupiter mission architecture studies1, we employ Lambert-arc solutions
for Earth-Jupiter transfers, effectively treating the spacecraft’s heliocentric leg as a two-body problem (Sun +
vehicle), and each hyperbolic planet exit/entry as a two-body problem (Earth/Jupiter + vehicle). Higher-order
multi-body perturbations are negligible for initial DV (delta-velocity) estimates and remain consistent with the
approach used historically by NASA. We iterated the poliastro Lambert-arc solver over 1-day timesteps in the
2030s to look for the lowest ejection DV solutions in the Sun reference frame with a 650 day Earth to Jupiter time of flight. 
650 days was arbritarily chosen to allow for the total mission time to total less than 2.5 years; we considered this a realistic target that remained a singificant improvement from state of the art Europa mission design. 



\subsection*{Tisserand plots and Jovian tour design}
The capture and start of Jovian orbit phasing followed a standard bi-elliptic capture burnset where Jupiter orbit insertion (JOI) is performed as close to Jupiter as is safe to maximize DV savings from the Oberth effect. To minimize time of flight, a baseline 1km/s JOI burn is performed, followed by a perijove raising maneuver that puts the Starship into an encounter Io. The set of Moons to flyby were calculated with a Tisserand plot. 

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{image.png}
    \caption{Jovian Tisserand plot. Each numbered contour represents all the possible orbits the spacecraft could be put onto after gravity assists with a given body.}
    \label{fig:placeholder}
\end{figure}

This graph shows the energy contours of trajectories that fly past the Galilean moons. Due to the conservation of energy, the spacecraft must leave the moon’s sphere of influence with as much velocity ($|V_{\infty}|$) relative to the moon as it had coming in. The magnitude, $|V_\infty|$, is unchanged by each flyby. It will however be rotated by an angle\cite{williams2012copernicus}:
$$
\theta=2asin\left(\frac{1}{V^2_{\infty}r_p/\mu+1}\right)
$$
Thus we can plot a set of orbits, each with the same $|V_\infty|$ relative to a moon at flyby as a contour of orbits with different perijoves and apijoves. Each assist rotates the $V_\infty$ vector to a new orbit along that contour. From this we can plot a path that brings the spacecraft to Europa with a low enough $V_\infty$ for an economical orbital insertion.

The rapid orbit of Io means there are more elliptical orbits that Starship can enter into that are resonant with Io -- essentially the phasing is easier. The high density of Io means the gravity assist deflection angle is greater than Europa and Callisto; the spacecraft can move down the Tisserand plot curve faster.
This led to trajectory design favoring flybys of Io lowering the apijove, followed by Ganymede perijove raising, and then final flybys of Europa and Ganymede for a Europa arrival with a$v_\infty$ less than 2km/s. The repeated flybys of a single moon are accomplished by requiring the flyby to send the Starship into an orbit a rational fraction of said Moon's orbital period -- the spacecraft enters into a resonance with the Moon. 

